    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nBack After 30 Years: 1969 Ford Mustang Mach 1 Left To Rot in a Yard Needs Total Restoration\n23 photos\nPhoto: eBay seller david159043\nThe Mach 1 continues to be one of the most intriguing restoration projects, and this 1969 specimen is living proof. The car has been sitting for over three decades, but this doesn’t mean it’s a wreck. It’s still a solid restoration candidate, and that’s specifically why it’s here.\nThe car was posted on eBay, where the owner is trying to let it go after keeping it for all these years in their yard.\nIt does suffer from obvious problems, but the seller didn’t share more information, so calling them would be a good place to start before making an offer.\nPhoto: eBay seller david159043 The first thing that probably catches everybody’s attention is the Mach 1 promise. This isn’t a regular Mustang, but a Mach 1 , and we all know what the Mach 1 represented in 1969.\nThis was the year when the Mach 1 came to be, almost immediately turning the Mustang GT into a configuration that barely made sense. The final production numbers for the 1969 model year speak for themselves, as Ford shipped 72,458 Mach 1s to buyers in the United States and only 5,396 Mustang GTs.\nFord didn’t necessarily consider this a problem, as the Mach 1 proving such a popular model almost overnight paved the way for growing sales in the following model years. However, the Mach 1 still couldn’t help the Mustang sales go up, with Ford ending the year with 299,824 cars.\nThe hardtop remained the most popular choice, and this wasn’t exactly surprising, considering it was also the most affordable version in the entire lineup. A six-cylinder-powered Mustang hardtop started at $2,618, while the newly introduced Mach 1 fastback carried a starting price tag of $3,122.\nPhoto: eBay seller david159043 The Mustang lineup included a plethora of engine choices for the 1969 model year, but the top choice was the Boss 429 Cobra Jet HO. It was a ridiculously expensive engine, as whoever wanted this engine had to pay no more, no less than $1,208 more on top of the Mustang price.\nThis Mustang Mach 1 isn’t fitted with this fantastic engine - and this isn’t a surprise because not many of these were built, so if you come across a genuine specimen, buy it without blinking because you could miss it - but it’s not a six-powered Mustang.\nThe Mach 1 could not be ordered with a six, but considering this is a project that’s been sitting for a long time - 30 years, according to the owner - discovering another engine under the hood wouldn’t be a surprise.\nThe unit in charge of putting the wheels in motion on this Mustang is a 351 Windsor, but we’re not getting any other specifics. For example, my first question would be whether this 351 is the original unit, so ask your mechanic to check everything in person before you even consider making an offer.\nThe engine is paired with an automatic transmission, but it’s obviously impossible to tell if they work. Considering this car has been sitting for some three decades - and by the looks of things, this happened under the clear sky, in the owner’s yard - I wouldn’t expect any good news.\nPhoto: eBay seller david159043 Otherwise, it’s pretty clear that this Mustang Mach 1 needs the obvious and sort of typical classic car work. It’s a project that suffers from unsurprising rust problems, though I would still go there in person to see everything live.\nThe Mach 1 landed online without too many specifics, so you should just ask for a walkaround video to obtain more information about this Mach 1. Nothing beats a live verification, but if you don’t want to leave on a long trip, a video that reveals more data would also come in handy.\nIt’s also unclear if anything big is missing, and this is critical information for anyone who wants to buy a Mach 1 for restoration. Bringing this car back to day-one specifications would be a challenge if too many parts are unavailable.\nPhoto: eBay seller david159043 The owner wants $12,000 for this Mach 1, but they also enabled the Make Offer option. This should be good news for potential buyers, as it’s a sign that the seller would also consider other offers, so you might be able to get a better deal if you’re committed to a purchase.\nThe eBay summary indicates a mileage of 35,000 miles, but it’s unclear if this is original to the car. A clean title is also available.\nThe vehicle is parked in Chicago, Illinois, and transportation will obviously be necessary, considering its shape. It’s also unclear if the wheels roll freely, but getting this car on your trailer shouldn’t be such a big deal with the right tools. The listing will go offline in 29 days, meaning that you have almost one month to call the seller, convince your significant other, and then go there in person to see this Mach 1 live.\nVia // eBay\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMach 1 Ford Ford Mach 1 Mustang 1969 Mach 1 Project Car\nAbout the author: Bogdan Popa\nBogdan keeps an eye on how technology is taking over the car world. His long-term goals are buying an 18-wheeler because he needs more space for his kid’s toys, and convincing Google and Apple that Android Auto and CarPlay deserve at least as much attention as their phones. Full profile\nUP NEXT Tiny Hot Hatch Just Sold for Porsche Money, Has a Punchy V6 Behind the Seats and a Fatty Widebody",
